# ===========================================================================
# RANKING CONSTANTS — the numbers that decide what gets injected.
#
# They live HERE, together, on purpose: recalibration must be a one-line diff,
# not archaeology across the file. Do NOT make either adaptive or per-query.
# One constant recalibrated offline is debuggable; a moving threshold is not —
# you could never tell whether a bad route came from the ranker or from the
# threshold in force that turn.
# ===========================================================================

# --- Absolute score floor --------------------------------------------------
# CALIBRATED, n=78 (60 positives = frontmatter trigger sentences with the skill
# name stripped; 18 negatives = real non-intent prompts from the hook log),
# ranked by the live daemon on the 1437-row table:
#     positives  top1  p05 0.741  p50 0.912  p95 0.959   (rank-1 correct 58/60)
#     negatives  top1  p50 0.375  p95 0.476  max 0.617
# The distributions DO NOT overlap on this set: positives bottom out at 0.741,
# noise tops out at 0.617 — a clean gap of 0.124. The noise p95, 0.476, retains
# 100% of positives.
#
# Retrieval quality was measured at 97% rank-1 correct, so a future reader
# knows the GATE was the defect, not the ranker.
#
# The previous 0.35 admitted the ENTIRE noise band 0.35-0.617, which is exactly
# where every bad injection observed in production sat: apple-reminders 0.380,
# yeet 0.450, grill-me 0.398, building-patch-tuesday 0.376, check-work 0.440.
#
# !! KNOWN TENSION, recorded rather than hidden !!
# The 60 positives above are each route's OWN indexed text with the name
# stripped — a near self-match, which is an upper bound on score. A separate
# run on 20 hand-written REALISTIC PARAPHRASES (queries not lifted from the
# index) scored far lower: min 0.315, p25 0.427, p50 0.464, max 0.779, and at
# 0.476 only 45% of them survive. Concretely, the paraphrase
# "my build is throwing an error and I need to find the root cause" routes to
# `diagnose` at 0.458 — correct, rank 1, and BELOW this floor. Whether 0.476 is
# right therefore depends on which population real prompts resemble. Both
# measurements are pinned in test/hook_gates.test.ts so the trade-off stays
# visible and a future recalibration starts from evidence, not from scratch.
#
# RESOLVED 2026-08-08 -> 0.40. The question above ("which population do real
# prompts resemble?") was settled by measurement: they resemble the PARAPHRASES.
# Six hand-written queries, every top-1 CORRECT, scored on the live daemon:
#     0.732 cloudflare-deploy   0.709 media-grab   0.530 mind
#     0.456 diagnose            0.450 arch-mind    0.428 git-workflow
# THREE OF SIX fall below 0.476 -- including `diagnose`, the case cited as proof
# the description re-embed worked. Live telemetry agreed: 6 of 13 real routing
# decisions rejected ALL 56-59 candidates.
#
# WHY 0.476 WAS WRONG: the n=78 calibration built its positives by taking each
# route's own indexed text and stripping the name -- a NEAR SELF-MATCH, which
# upper-bounds the score (p05 0.741). That population does not occur in
# production. It manufactured an apparent 0.124 separation gap that realistic
# queries do not have (paraphrase p50 0.464 vs noise max 0.617 -- heavy overlap).
# The threshold is therefore a BACKSTOP, not the primary filter; the intent gate
# (which rejects harness-shaped and contentless turns before embedding) does the
# real work. That was the original prediction and the flawed calibration briefly
# appeared to refute it.
MIN_SCORE = _env_float("MIND_NERVE_MIN_SCORE", 0.40)

# --- Relative margin gate: MEASURED AND REJECTED ---------------------------
# A Lowe's-ratio gate (reject when top2/top1 > cut) was specified, then dropped
# on evidence. Over the same n=78 calibration:
#     positives  ratio  p50 0.782  p95 1.000
#     negatives  ratio  p50 0.973  p05 0.857
# The bands overlap almost completely, so ANY cutoff rejects a large slice of
# CORRECT routes. An earlier cut of 0.85 looked perfect on n=7 and does not
# survive n=78. This is precisely the margin-gate-versus-near-duplicates
# conflict that was predicted: legitimate near-ties are COMMON here, not rare.
#
# The ratio is still COMPUTED and LOGGED to telemetry — it is potentially
# useful signal for a future calibration — but it never gates anything.
# Absolute score separates; the ratio does not.
APPLY_RATIO_GATE = False

INTENT_GATE_ON = _env_flag("MIND_NERVE_INTENT_GATE", True)
